  "image_name": "1ad49711f1879af6bfabe93a8c32a9d6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several therapeutic strategies aimed at reducing mast-cell–driven disease. Overall, it shows that mast cells can be controlled either by directly preventing their activation and degranulation, by blocking downstream nerve signaling, or by altering the microbiota.\n\nThe largest section focuses on mast cell stabilizers. One approach inhibits degranulation itself, preventing release of granule contents from the mast cell. Other approaches block activation pathways: anti-IgE antibodies suppress IgE-dependent mast cell activation, while anti-Siglec-8 antibodies appear to inhibit IgE-independent activation. A separate strategy uses anti-c-KIT antibodies to deplete mast cells altogether rather than simply calming them.\n\nAnother section highlights blockade of afferent nerve activation or sensitization. A branching sensory nerve is labeled with targets such as 5-HT3, H1R, and PAR2, indicating that antagonists to serotonin and histamine receptors, as well as protease inhibitors, may reduce nerve-mediated symptoms triggered downstream of mast cell mediators.\n\nThe final section presents microbiota-targeted interventions. A stylized microbial community is paired with probiotics, antibiotics, and fecal microbiota transplantation, suggesting that changing gut microbes may indirectly influence mast cell activity or related inflammatory responses.",
